    <section class="content-section" aria-labelledby="tola-gram-heading">
      <h2 id="tola-gram-heading">Tola and gram: which unit is your rate in?</h2>
      <p>In Pakistan and much of South Asia, gold is traditionally weighed and priced by the tola, while many shops, banks and international markets quote prices per gram or per 10 grams. A tola is not 10 grams: it is 11.6638038 grams, defined as 180 grains, with one grain equal to 64.79891 milligrams. Smaller jewellery is often weighed in masha and ratti: 12 masha make a tola and 8 ratti make a masha, so a tola is 96 ratti.</p>
      <p>Because the units differ, the same day's price can look very different from shop to shop. Choose the unit your rate is quoted in, and the calculator converts everything to grams before working out the value. The result also shows the equivalent rate per tola, per 10 grams and per gram, so you can compare quotes that use different units.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="content-section" aria-labelledby="karat-heading">
      <h2 id="karat-heading">Karat and purity</h2>
      <p>A karat measures how much of an item is pure gold, out of 24 parts. The calculator uses purity = karat ÷ 24:</p>
      <ul class="content-list">
        <li><strong>24K:</strong> pure gold. Gold sold as 24K is normally hallmarked 999, or 99.9% gold.</li>
        <li><strong>22K:</strong> 22/24 = 91.7% gold, the most common purity for jewellery in Pakistan and India, often marked 916.</li>
        <li><strong>21K:</strong> 21/24 = 87.5% gold, widely used in the Gulf and for some local jewellery.</li>
        <li><strong>18K:</strong> 18/24 = 75% gold, common for jewellery set with stones.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>The rest of the metal is usually silver, copper or zinc, which makes the jewellery harder. When your rate is for one karat and the item is another, the calculator scales the price by the ratio of the two purities. For example, a 22K item priced from a 24K rate is worth 22/24 of the 24K amount at the same weight.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="content-section" aria-labelledby="shop-price-heading">
      <h2 id="shop-price-heading">Why shop prices differ from the gold value</h2>
      <p>The calculator's gold value is what the metal in the item is worth at the rate you entered. A jeweller's quote is usually higher, for a few reasons:</p>
      <ul class="content-list">
        <li><strong>Making charges:</strong> the cost of turning gold into jewellery, charged either as a percentage of the gold value or as a fixed amount per gram or per tola. Intricate designs usually cost more.</li>
        <li><strong>Wastage:</strong> some shops add a charge for metal lost while the piece was made.</li>
        <li><strong>Kaat:</strong> when you sell or exchange gold, a jeweller may deduct kaat for impurity, solder or wastage, so you receive less than the gold value.</li>
        <li><strong>Stones and other materials:</strong> their weight is not gold, so ask for the net gold weight.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Enter the making charges a shop quotes to see the total, and compare the gold value line between shops: if two quotes have the same gold value, the difference is in the charges.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="content-section" aria-labelledby="todays-rate-heading">
      <h2 id="todays-rate-heading">Use today's rate from your sarafa association or jeweller</h2>
      <p>Gold prices change during the day and differ between cities and countries. This calculator does not show or fetch live prices. Take the day's rate from your local sarafa association or your jeweller, note which unit and karat it is for, and enter it here. Your rate and weights stay in your browser.</p>
    </section>
